This code uses the `libgpiod` library to interact with the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi. It sets up the TRIG pin as an output and the ECHO pin as an input, and uses the `gpiod_line_request_input` and `gpiod_line_request_output` functions to request the lines. It then sets the TRIG pin to a high value for 10 ms, which triggers the ultrasonic sensor to send out a pulse. The ECHO pin is then set to a low value, and the program waits for the echo pulse to arrive.

When the echo pulse arrives, the `echo_callback` function is called, which reads the pulse width and checks if it is within the valid range. If the pulse width is valid, the program converts the pulse width to meters and appends the reading to the log file.

The program also installs a SIGINT/SIGTERM handler to cleanly shut down the program when it receives a signal. The handler sets the `running` flag to `false`, which causes the program to exit the main loop and clean up the GPIO lines and the log file.

Note that this code assumes that the ultrasonic sensor is connected to the Raspberry Pi with the TRIG pin connected to GPIO17 (BCM) and the ECHO pin connected to GPIO18 (BCM) through a 1 kΩ/2 kΩ voltage divider to keep the Pi input at ~3.3 V.

