This code uses the `libgpiod` library to interact with the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi. It sets up the trigger and echo pins as output, and the buzzer pin as output. It then sets the trigger pin high for 10 µs, and sets the buzzer pin low. It then sets the echo pin as input and sets up a rising/falling edge alert callback for the echo pin. The callback function is called whenever the echo pin changes state, and it records the start and end times of the echo pulse, computes the distance, and sets the buzzer pin high if the distance is under 20 cm. The program also installs a SIGINT handler to cleanly terminate the driver library and exit on interrupt.

