This code uses the `libgpiod` library to interact with the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi. It sets up two GPIO lines, one for input and one for output, and configures them as inputs and outputs respectively. It then enters a loop where it reads the value of the input pin every 10 milliseconds and checks if it has changed from low to high. If it has, it sets the output pin to high and prints a message to the console. It also uses a debounce time of 200 milliseconds to prevent false positives.

The code also handles SIGINT and SIGTERM signals by turning off the LED and closing the log file before exiting.

Note that this code assumes that the SainSmart TTP223B capacitive touch sensor is connected to GPIO4 (BCM, physical pin 7) and the LED is connected to GPIO17 (BCM, physical pin 11). If your sensor and LED are connected to different pins, you will need to modify the code accordingly.

