This code uses the `libgpiod` library to access the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi, and the `libzmq` library to communicate with the ZeroMQ socket. It also uses the `pthread` library to create two separate threads for the sound sensor and alarm node modes.

The `gpio_init()` function initializes the GPIO bus and maps the GPIO registers into memory. The `gpio_set_mode()` and `gpio_set_value()` functions are used to set the mode and value of the GPIO pins. The `gpio_cleanup()` function is used to release the GPIO lines and chip.

The `zmq_init()` function initializes the ZeroMQ context and socket, and the `zmq_cleanup()` function is used to release the socket and context. The `zmq_send()` and `zmq_recv()` functions are used to send and receive messages on the ZeroMQ socket.

The `alarm_node()` function subscribes to the ZeroMQ socket for the `SOUND_ALERTS` topic, and upon receiving a message, it lights the alarm LED for exactly 1 second. The `sound_sensor()` function polls the LM393 sound sensor on its digital output pin connected to GPIO17 (BCM) every 10 ms, and treats a logic 0 on this active-low pin as a sound event. It then publishes an alert on the ZeroMQ socket bound to `tcp://*:5555` using the `SOUND_ALERTS` topic.

The `main()` function creates two separate threads for the sound sensor and alarm node modes, and joins them before cleaning up the GPIO and ZeroMQ resources. It also checks the command-line arguments to determine which mode to run in.

